{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you came in this morning feeling confused and shaky?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I didn’t eat breakfast and didn’t take my insulin. Then I started sweating and feeling really weak."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We checked your labs and found your potassium was quite high at 6.2, and your sodium was low. We gave you something called Kayexalate in the ER to help bring the potassium down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. Is that dangerous? I’ve been eating a lot of bananas and oranges lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, those are high in potassium. With your liver condition and the spironolactone you're on, it can build up. That likely contributed to the high level. Your EKG didn’t show any dangerous changes, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I also felt confused. Was that from the potassium or my liver?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We considered several things—your liver, infection, or electrolytes. But since you hadn’t eaten or taken insulin, low blood sugar is the most likely cause. Your mental status is much better now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I did finish my Bactrim for the UTI yesterday. Could that have affected my potassium?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, Bactrim can raise potassium, especially with other factors. Since you’ve completed the course, we’re stopping it. That will help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my sodium? It was low too, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2193-11-26 18:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your sodium was 132. That’s common with cirrhosis. We’re not giving IV fluids because your body holds onto water. It’s improved to 138 now, which is normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2193-11-26 19:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also scheduled your CyberKnife treatment for tomorrow morning at 9:30. It’s important to continue that for your liver tumor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2193-11-26 19:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’ve been worried about missing it. Is it safe to go home after?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2193-11-26 19:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s outpatient. You’ll be monitored afterward. We’ll discharge you tomorrow once we confirm your labs are stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your repeat labs show your potassium is now 4.6 and sodium is normal. You’re doing well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What changes should I make to my meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re reducing your spironolactone from 100 mg to 25 mg daily and adding Lasix 20 mg to help balance your fluids and protect your potassium. And stop the Bactrim—you’re done with it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Any other warnings I should know about?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call your doctor or go to the ER if you have fever over 101, chest pain, shortness of breath, or abdominal pain. And if your blood sugar drops below 70, eat some juice or candy right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll keep eating less banana, I promise."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good start. We’ll also send a visiting nurse to check on you at home. Make sure to keep all your follow-up appointments."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’re discharging you soon. Just sign the papers, and the nurse will go over your updated medication list one more time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will my confusion come back if I skip meals?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could. Skipping insulin or meals can drop your sugar and affect your thinking. Please eat regularly and take your insulin as prescribed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll be more careful. Thanks for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing great. Just keep up with your diet, meds, and appointments. We’re here if you need us."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2193-11-27 10:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. See you at the next visit."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2193-11-26 18:11:00",
    "admission_end": "2193-11-27 16:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient, a woman with cirrhosis from chronic hepatitis C, hepatocellular carcinoma, and type 2 diabetes, was admitted after presenting with confusion and shakiness. Initial labs revealed hyperkalemia (K 6.2) and hyponatremia (Na 132), likely due to dietary indiscretion, spironolactone, and insulin omission. She was treated with Kayexalate in the ED, and her potassium normalized. Her confusion resolved with improved intake and glucose control. She completed a course of Bactrim for a UTI, which was discontinued. She underwent CyberKnife therapy as part of her outpatient cancer treatment plan. She was discharged on reduced-dose spironolactone, new Lasix, and continued home medications with education on low-potassium diet and symptom monitoring. Her condition was stable at discharge.",
    "problems": [
      "Hyperpotassemia",
      "Urinary tract infection, site not specified",
      "Chronic hepatitis C without mention of hepatic coma",
      "Cirrhosis of liver without mention of alcohol",
      "Other convulsions",
      "Hyposmolality and/or hyponatremia",
      "Unspecified psychosis",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Essential and other specified forms of tremor",
      "Calculus of gallbladder without mention of cholecystitis, without mention of obstruction",
      "Nontoxic uninodular goiter",
      "Anemia, unspecified",
      "Disorder of bone and cartilage, unspecified",
      "Personal history of peptic ulcer disease",
      "Other postprocedural status"
    ]
  }
}
